1
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:14,810
Hello and welcome to this massive open online course, Introduction to BioRisk Management.

2
00:00:14,810 --> 00:00:20,730
I am your instructor Kenneth, and in this segment we will be discussing about samples

3
00:00:20,730 --> 00:00:26,570
and specifically referring to hospital derived samples.

4
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:33,530
Now in your role as a laboratory technician in a biosafety level 3 laboratory, you will

5
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:38,330
be required to analyze samples from different locations.

6
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:44,490
Samples which are derived from researchers which consist of exonic opeocultures are easy

7
00:00:44,490 --> 00:00:47,930
to analyze because the risk can be assessed very easily.

8
00:00:47,930 --> 00:00:53,090
We are aware of the biological agent and we are also aware of any modifications which have

9
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,970
been made to that biological agent.

10
00:00:55,970 --> 00:01:01,250
The situation changes slightly when we have to analyze samples derived from patients.

11
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:06,370
These may be in the form of swabs, serum samples, blood, urine or stools.

12
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:13,130
When samples are received from hospitals, they may consist of more than one biological agent

13
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:16,450
within a single sample.

14
00:01:16,450 --> 00:01:18,610
How do we address this risk?

15
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:23,330
So in the case of hospital samples, we assume the highest possible risk.

16
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:28,690
Now when samples are received from hospitals, they will be shipped in a special packing

17
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:30,250
container.

18
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:35,930
This is an example of a bag in which the sample will be shipped and this bag will

19
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:45,730
be sealed, it will be locked with a tie and then shipped in a container which may contain

20
00:01:45,730 --> 00:01:55,090
ice or some other refrigerant such as carbon dioxide, CO2 ice, what we call as dry ice.

21
00:01:55,090 --> 00:01:58,730
Okay so when you receive these samples, the first thing which you need to do is to ensure

22
00:01:58,730 --> 00:02:04,450
that the outer packing material is thoroughly sterilized with a sterilant.

23
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:10,850
In our case we use 70% ethanol, we wait for around 30 seconds and we open the bag after

24
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:11,850
30 seconds.

25
00:02:11,850 --> 00:02:16,770
The 30 second period is known as the contact time.

26
00:02:16,770 --> 00:02:23,570
Once you have opened your bag, you will obtain the sample, I am going to take out

27
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:28,700
a sample from this bag.

28
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:33,620
The sample will be generally shipped in a tube like this.

29
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:39,420
This is a sample containing tube, I cannot reveal the details of the patient as this

30
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:42,900
is a patient derived sample.

31
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:49,660
Now this sample is basically a serum sample you can see in the tube and you can note

32
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:56,140
that there is a small ward of bandage over here.

33
00:02:56,140 --> 00:03:00,780
Now there is a purpose for this specific ward of bandage here.

34
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:07,900
So when I open the sample in the biological safety cabinet, any of the serum which drips

35
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:12,020
from this particular tube will be trapped by this ward.

36
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:16,260
It will not spill onto my gloves and cause a breach of containment.

37
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:20,620
So this is one of the key aspects of transporting samples from hospitals.

38
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:25,020
They must be accompanied with the proper transport security.

39
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:31,260
Now the technician who has drawn the sample or the doctor at the hospital has to ensure

40
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:37,980
that the sample is properly labeled and properly bagged so as to ensure that the transport

41
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:42,580
agent or the one who transport the sample to the hospital as well as the technician

42
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:48,740
who works in the laboratory is basically protected from the biological agent.

43
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:54,860
Now what happens when the samples come into our lab?

44
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:58,380
Basically there are two types of samples which come in, the first in which we have to culture

45
00:03:58,380 --> 00:03:59,380
the biological agent.

46
00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:07,420
So in that case we practice the extreme set of precautions and we assess the risk in

47
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:08,820
terms of a live culture.

48
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:14,100
So the sample for instance bacterial samples may need to be transferred onto media.

49
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:18,860
In the case of serum samples which got to be analyzed for instance nucleic acids or

50
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:23,980
in this case we are analyzing samples from COVID-19 patients or suspected patients.

51
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:28,740
We move on to the next step which is inactivation of the sample.

52
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:32,460
Now for inactivation of the samples we have to follow the standard operating procedure

53
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:34,460
for that particular biological agent.

54
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:42,700
In this case of COVID-19 it involves elevating the sample to a temperature of 60 to 70 degrees

55
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:50,300
in a thermal block and after a period of one hour the sample is basically inactivated.

56
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:55,820
Now remember that all these procedures must be conducted within a biological safety cabinet

57
00:04:55,820 --> 00:05:02,260
with the appropriate PPEs and in accordance with the administrative procedures or administrative

58
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:03,940
controls.

59
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:09,260
Once the sample is inactivated we can process to the next step which is the extraction

60
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:14,860
of the nucleic acid which must also be conducted in a biological safety cabinet.

61
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:21,660
Once the nucleic acid is extracted the sample is ready for processing which in terms of the

62
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:23,740
analysis.

63
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:30,460
So in our case for COVID-19 we use a thermal cycler, a real time thermal cycler which will

64
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:36,180
analyze the sample for the specific biological agent.

65
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:42,980
Now all these aspects need to be integrated into your sample preparation strategy.

66
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:46,780
You must assess the risk of the procedure itself.

67
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:51,340
So coming back to what we have learned, we have a risk assessment.

68
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:56,900
So we assess the risk both by the biological agent, in the case of a sample from hospital

69
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:02,100
patients we assume the highest risk, we then apply the specific controls.

70
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:07,820
So in this case I am in a biological safety level 3 laboratory and I am applying the pertinent

71
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:13,380
engineering controls as well as the personal protective equipment which I am wearing and

72
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:17,420
the engineering controls which is this unit itself.

73
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:22,060
These are some of the key points which we need to take into consideration when managing

74
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:28,860
biological agents arrive from known as well as unknown samples.

75
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:33,380
Thank you very much for watching this short segment.

76
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:36,340
I will discuss further in my lecture.

77
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:38,260
In the meantime, stay bio safe.
